---
import LessonLayout from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import ClaimLabel from '../../components/ClaimLabel.astro';
import Cite from '../../components/learn/Cite.astro';
import SimPanel from '../../components/learn/SimPanel.astro';
import { ENTITIES, ENTITY_INFO, type EntityId } from '../../lib/engines/ledger/model';
import { flowSvg, flowView } from '../../lib/learn/flow-view';
import { FLOW_CATALOG, FLOW_ENTITIES, FLOW_OPENING_TEXT, FLOW_STEPS, flowCounterRows, flowsOpening } from '../../lib/learn/flows';
import { FLOW_SOURCES, LESSONS_REVIEWED, LESSONS_REVIEWED_LABEL, numberer } from '../../lib/learn/lesson-sources';
import { LESSON_CAPITAL_RATIO } from '../../lib/learn/banks';
import { counters } from '../../lib/learn/sim';
import { href } from '../../lib/url';
import '../../styles/learn.css';

const n = numberer(FLOW_SOURCES);
const opening = flowsOpening();
const names = Object.fromEntries(ENTITIES.map((e) => [e, ENTITY_INFO[e].name])) as Record<EntityId, string>;
---
<LessonLayout
  pageTitle="How Money Flows: Payments, Taxes and the Fed on Balance Sheets"
  image="lesson-how-money-flows"
  about={['Payment systems', 'Bank reserves', 'Treasury General Account', 'Open market operations']}
  title="How Money Flows"
  description="Follow wages, spending, lending, taxes, government spending, Treasury borrowing and Federal Reserve purchases between households, businesses, banks, the Treasury and the Fed, on balance sheets that always balance."
  lead="Every payment is a change on at least two balance sheets. Follow money between households, a business, two banks, the Treasury, the Federal Reserve and financial markets, step by step, then try any transaction yourself."
  reviewed={LESSONS_REVIEWED}
  minutes={15}
>
  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="layers">
    <h2 id="layers">Two layers of money</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /></p>
    <p>
      Households and businesses pay each other with bank deposits. Banks pay each other with
      reserves, their accounts at the Federal Reserve, and a Fedwire payment between them is
      "immediate, final, and irrevocable once processed."<Cite ids={['fed-fedwire']} number={n} />
      The government has an account there too: the Treasury General Account, "the U.S.
      government's operating account."<Cite ids={['treasury-tga']} number={n} /> Watch those three
      totals (deposits, reserves and the Treasury's account) as you step through.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SimPanel lesson="flows" heading="Follow the money" entities={FLOW_ENTITIES} opening={opening} openingText={FLOW_OPENING_TEXT} steps={FLOW_STEPS.length} counters={flowCounterRows(counters(opening))} actions={FLOW_CATALOG.map((a) => ({ id: a.id, label: a.label }))} capitalRatio={LESSON_CAPITAL_RATIO} flowSvg={flowSvg(flowView(null), names, 'mf-flow')} />

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="steps">
    <h2 id="steps">What happens at each step</h2>
    <p class="small muted">Amounts are illustrative. The ledger checks after every step that each balance sheet balances and that every asset is someone else's liability.</p>
    <ol>
      {FLOW_STEPS.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.title}.</strong> {s.text}</li>)}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="government">
    <h2 id="government">The government's account and bank reserves</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /></p>
    <p>
      Taxes and government spending move reserves. In the words of a Federal Reserve governor,
      "when tax payments are made to the Treasury, we debit the payer's bank reserves and credit
      the Treasury's TGA account," and "when the Treasury makes a payment, we debit the TGA and
      credit the recipient bank's reserve account."<Cite ids={['fed-waller-2025']} number={n} />
      So, all else equal, "reserves adjust to TGA fluctuations, in the opposite direction of the
      TGA."<Cite ids={['feds-2025-tga']} number={n} /> Selling a Treasury security to the public does
      the same as a tax payment: the buyer's deposit and its bank's reserves move into the
      Treasury's account.<Cite ids={['treasury-tga', 'fed-liabilities']} number={n} />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="fed">
    <h2 id="fed">How the Federal Reserve adds reserves</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /></p>
    <p>
      "When the Federal Reserve adds reserves to the financial system, it does so by buying
      Treasury securities from private markets and crediting reserve accounts of banks."<Cite ids={['fed-iorb-faq']} number={n} />
      When the seller is not a bank, "it creates new bank deposits by crediting the reserve
      account of the depository institution at which the nonbank seller has an account, and
      then the depository institution credits the deposit account of the nonbank
      seller."<Cite ids={['feds-2022']} number={n} /> In practice the Fed buys from dealers and pays
      through their banks;<Cite ids={['fed-explained']} number={n} /> the simulation sells
      straight from the investor to keep the steps short.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two transactions in the free mode drain reserves instead. A cash withdrawal comes out of
      the bank's vault, and when the bank buys more currency from the Fed it pays "with a debit
      to its Federal Reserve account";<Cite ids={['fed-liabilities']} number={n} /> the simulation
      shows both parts at once. And a money market fund can lend to the Fed overnight through
      its reverse repo facility, open to "2a-7 money market funds, banks, and
      government-sponsored enterprises," which "reduces bank reserve liabilities on the Federal
      Reserve's balance sheet and increases RRP liabilities by an equal offsetting
      amount."<Cite ids={['nyfed-rrp']} number={n} />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="simplified">
    <h2 id="simplified">What the simulation leaves out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>One of each kind of institution stands in for the whole economy, and the amounts are illustrative.</li>
      <li>Wages, purchases, interest and taxes change equity directly: no goods, income statements or tax rules are modeled.</li>
      <li>The Fed buys from the investor directly; real purchases go through dealers and their banks.</li>
      <li>A cash withdrawal shows its net effect only: the bank's vault cash is restocked from the Fed at once.</li>
      <li>Payments settle at once, with no intraday credit and no clearing banks.</li>
      <li>The capital rule you can switch on in free mode (equity of at least 5 percent of assets) is a hypothetical one for teaching, not a regulatory minimum. <a href={href('learn/how-banks-create-money/')}>How Banks Create Money</a> describes the real minimums.</li>
      <li>A write-off goes straight to the bank's equity and also cancels the borrower's debt. Real banks charge losses against an allowance built up through provisions, and a charged-off loan is still owed unless the bank forgives it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="key-ideas" aria-labelledby="key-ideas">
    <h2 id="key-ideas">Key ideas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every payment changes at least two balance sheets, and every one still balances afterward.</li>
      <li>The public pays with deposits; banks settle with reserves; the Treasury keeps its account at the Fed.</li>
      <li>A payment within one bank moves no reserves; a payment between banks does.</li>
      <li>Taxes and Treasury borrowing move reserves into the Treasury's account; government spending moves them back out.</li>
      <li>Fed purchases of securities add reserves, and when the seller is not a bank they add deposits too.</li>
      <li>Bank lending creates deposits and repayment destroys them; see <a href={href('learn/how-banks-create-money/')}>How Banks Create Money</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="related">
    <h2 id="related">Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={href('learn/what-is-money/')}>What Is Money?</a>: deposits, reserves and Treasury bills compared.</li>
      <li><a href={href('learn/how-banks-create-money/')}>How Banks Create Money</a>: a loan from start to finish.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="sources">
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <p class="small">Every source was checked on {LESSONS_REVIEWED_LABEL}.</p>
    <ol class="source-list small">
      {FLOW_SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li id={`src-${s.id}`}>{s.publisher}, <a href={s.url}>{s.title}</a>, {s.date}.</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>
</LessonLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/learn/flows';
</script>
